WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.154 --> 00:00:14.218
leaving the Island of the Lotus Eaters, Odysseus and his men sailed on for another day.

2
00:00:14.598 --> 00:00:17.080
Their hunger and thirst were overwhelming, but they endured.

3
00:00:17.701 --> 00:00:21.564
Early the next evening as the sun settled into the sea, they came to an island that had

4
00:00:21.644 --> 00:00:24.247
wild grapes, barley, and goats in abundance.

5
00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:27.049
They caught the goats easily for they had no fear of men.

6
00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:30.372
In the distance, another island could be seen near the horizon.

7
00:00:31.088 --> 00:00:34.771
Odysseus and his men saw the smoke of many cooking fires rising from this island.

8
00:00:34.791 --> 00:00:40.676
"Tomorrow," called Odysseus to his men, "I will cross the sea and seek out those inhabitants.

9
00:00:41.036 --> 00:00:44.639
If they are a civilized people then perhaps we can trade some of our wine for their cured

10
00:00:44.679 --> 00:00:45.400
meats and bread."

11
00:00:47.041 --> 00:00:51.545
The following morning, Odysseus' ship crossed the sea and set off for the island near the horizon.

12
00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:56.449
Odysseus was worried because he did not know if the creatures were civilized or savage, and if they

13
00:00:56.469 --> 00:00:57.410
would respect the law.

14
00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:01.973
Therefore, when they arrived at the island, he did not dare take all his men onto it.

15
00:01:02.453 --> 00:01:04.553
He only chose twelve of his best to accompany him.

16
00:01:05.473 --> 00:01:06.654
The rest stayed with the ship.

17
00:01:07.154 --> 00:01:11.255
Odysseus and his men took many skins of wine with them, and followed a path from the beach.

18
00:01:11.695 --> 00:01:13.736
It led to a cave that was located in the cliffs.

19
00:01:14.336 --> 00:01:17.477
The cave was unoccupied because the owner was out tending his flocks.

20
00:01:18.157 --> 00:01:23.658
Inside, Odysseus and his men discovered many blocks of cheese, and two pens overflowing with lambs and kids.

21
00:01:24.639 --> 00:01:29.820
Odysseus' men tried to convince Odysseus to steal the cheese, lambs and kids, but Odysseus refused.

22
00:01:30.324 --> 00:01:32.346
"We have come as friends, not as thieves.

23
00:01:32.766 --> 00:01:36.569
We will give the wine as a gift to our host and we will accept whatever hospitality they

24
00:01:36.589 --> 00:01:37.569
will give us in return."

25
00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.411
The men were not pleased to hear this, but they obeyed.

26
00:01:41.032 --> 00:01:45.955
Odysseus and his men passed the time by lighting a fire, making sacrifices to the gods, and eating

27
00:01:45.995 --> 00:01:46.736
some of the cheese.

28
00:01:47.396 --> 00:01:52.140
Towards evening a giant human-like creature, with just a single eye in the middle of his forehead, returned

29
00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:52.820
with his flocks.

30
00:01:53.421 --> 00:01:57.303
It was a cyclops and he carried a load of firewood and dropped it near the mouth of

31
00:01:57.324 --> 00:01:57.744
the cave.

32
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.561
The cyclops then drove his flocks into the cave.

33
00:02:01.162 --> 00:02:04.804
Once the goats and sheep were inside, he rolled a huge rock to block the entrance of the

34
00:02:04.844 --> 00:02:08.706
cave. This rock was so big that it would take twenty oxen to move it.

35
00:02:09.847 --> 00:02:13.529
Odysseus and his men were a little frightened by the sight and size of the creature, so they

36
00:02:13.589 --> 00:02:15.090
silently watched him milk his sheep.

37
00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:20.513
But soon the cyclops' gaze fell upon the strangers, "Who has entered the home of Polypemus?"

38
00:02:21.354 --> 00:02:24.916
Odysseus bowed and replied most humbly, "Kind sir, we are Greeks.

39
00:02:25.216 --> 00:02:29.198
We were part of that great expedition that recently won much glory in the conquest of Troy.

40
00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:31.399
Now we are returning home.

41
00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:34.061
We ask for your hospitality in the name of the gods."

42
00:02:34.801 --> 00:02:35.942
Polypemus did not answer.

43
00:02:36.262 --> 00:02:39.044
Instead he reached out his hand and seized two of Odysseus' men.

44
00:02:39.464 --> 00:02:42.065
He hurled them against the side of the cave as if they were dolls.

45
00:02:42.506 --> 00:02:46.608
The men landed motionless on the floor, and Polypemus devoured them with great relish.

46
00:02:47.989 --> 00:02:50.830
After eating the men, Polypemus stretched out on the floor to sleep.

47
00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:54.032
Odysseus was tempted to plunge his sword into him as he slept.

48
00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.004
However, he decided that killing Polypemus would lead them all to certain destruction, because no one except Polypemus

49
00:03:00.024 --> 00:03:02.806
was strong enough to move the rock that blocked the entrance of the cave.

50
00:03:03.586 --> 00:03:07.469
The next morning, the cyclops ate two more of Odysseus' men for breakfast.

51
00:03:08.189 --> 00:03:12.833
After feasting on the men, the cyclops moved the rock away from the entrance, drove out his flocks,

52
00:03:13.153 --> 00:03:15.114
and carefully put the rock back in its place.

53
00:03:15.574 --> 00:03:19.877
While the cyclops was gone, Odysseus planned to avenge the cyclops for the murder of his men.

54
00:03:20.558 --> 00:03:23.600
First, he asked his remaining eight men to make a spear from a staff.

55
00:03:24.076 --> 00:03:26.958
They sharpened the end of the staff and hid it under the straw on the floor.

56
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:31.482
That evening after the cyclops returned home, Odysseus approached him.

57
00:03:31.742 --> 00:03:36.166
He handed the cyclops a bowl of wine, saying, "Before you eat more of my men, drink some

58
00:03:36.206 --> 00:03:39.528
wine. You will find that the wine will go well with your meal of human flesh."

59
00:03:40.329 --> 00:03:42.071
Polypemus took the wine and drank some.

60
00:03:42.551 --> 00:03:46.294
He had never tasted it before, so he was hugely delighted and he called for more.

61
00:03:46.934 --> 00:03:48.856
Odysseus happily kept refilling his bowl.

62
00:03:49.496 --> 00:03:53.680
This pleased the giant so much that he promised Odysseus that he should be the last one devoured.

63
00:03:54.216 --> 00:03:58.437
"Since you are the last of the humans to be eaten, I would like to know your name,"

64
00:03:58.937 --> 00:03:59.798
inquired Polypemus.

65
00:03:59.818 --> 00:04:03.279
"My name," replied Odysseus, "is Nobody."

66
00:04:04.699 --> 00:04:07.460
Drinking wine on an empty stomach soon made Polypemus sleepy.

67
00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:09.520
As soon as he lay down he fell asleep.

68
00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.641
Odysseus took the spear from under the straw and plunged it into the monster's eye.

69
00:04:14.042 --> 00:04:15.882
The cyclops awoke, howling in pain.

70
00:04:16.782 --> 00:04:20.623
The howling was so loud that the other cyclops from the island came to Polypemus' cave.

71
00:04:21.184 --> 00:04:24.333
Outside the cave, they shouted, "What's wrong?"

72
00:04:24.933 --> 00:04:27.595
Polypemus replied, "I am blind!

73
00:04:27.956 --> 00:04:29.297
It was Nobody that hurt me!

74
00:04:29.597 --> 00:04:30.397
Nobody did it!"

75
00:04:31.578 --> 00:04:36.902
The cyclops outside laughed and said, "If nobody has hurt you, then it is your fate to be

76
00:04:36.962 --> 00:04:38.904
hurt!" and they went away.

77
00:04:40.285 --> 00:04:43.888
The next morning, Polypemus awoke and still could not see anything from his one eye.

78
00:04:44.228 --> 00:04:47.690
But he managed to roll away the huge stone and waited by the entrance with the hope of

79
00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:48.571
catching Odysseus.

80
00:04:49.848 --> 00:04:54.531
But Odysseus had anticipated this and had ordered his men to tie themselves underneath the largest rams.

81
00:04:55.511 --> 00:05:00.094
As Polypemus let his sheep out of the cave, he checked only their tops and so Odysseus and

82
00:05:00.134 --> 00:05:00.895
his men escaped.

83
00:05:02.496 --> 00:05:06.498
When they had gotten a fair distance from the cave, Odysseus and his men untied themselves from the

84
00:05:06.538 --> 00:05:09.940
rams and took many sheep down to the shore, to where their ship waited.

85
00:05:11.181 --> 00:05:13.482
The men quickly put the sheep on board, and set sail.

86
00:05:13.963 --> 00:05:20.690
Odysseus shouted, "Cyclops, the gods have punished you for your uncivilized deeds, and it was I, Odysseus who

87
00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:21.930
has left you blind."

88
00:05:22.891 --> 00:05:24.251
Polypemus howled with rage.

89
00:05:24.492 --> 00:05:26.753
He seized a rock and threw it in the direction of the voice.

90
00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:28.513
The rock just missed the ship.

91
00:05:29.174 --> 00:05:33.675
Odysseus waited until the ship had reached a safer distance, and then he taunted the giant again.

92
00:05:35.196 --> 00:05:40.618
As Odysseus and his men sailed away, Polypemus called out to his father: "Hear me, Poseidon.

93
00:05:41.279 --> 00:05:43.860
If I am truly your son, grant me this wish.

94
00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.381
May Odysseus never reach his home in Ithaca.

95
00:05:46.962 --> 00:05:52.084
If however, the gods decided he was destined to reach his native land, then let him suffer.

96
00:05:52.485 --> 00:05:57.687
Let him come home late, with all his comrades dead, in someone else's ship, and let him find

97
00:05:57.767 --> 00:05:58.988
troubles in his own house."

98
00:06:00.109 --> 00:06:03.631
Poseidon heard his son, and he saw Odysseus' disdain for the cyclops.

99
00:06:04.091 --> 00:06:08.373
A great rage filled Poseidon and he called to his son, "Oh, my son.

100
00:06:08.533 --> 00:06:11.415
Odysseus' destiny is indeed fixed in the stars.

101
00:06:11.975 --> 00:06:15.730
His fate is to return home, but the stars, like the gods, are immortal.

102
00:06:16.251 --> 00:06:21.316
He will come home late, his comrades will all have died, he will arrive in another person's ship,

103
00:06:21.676 --> 00:06:23.218
and he will have trouble in his house.

104
00:06:23.718 --> 00:06:26.982
My son, I swear, Odysseus will suffer."
